          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            When
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
            is used to model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mechanics"} mechanics
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            encodes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mechanical+system"} mechanical system
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectomorphism"} symplectomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\phi \;\colon\; (X_1,\omega_1) \to (X_2, \omega_2)
          %p
            encodes a process undergone by this system, for instance the time evolution induced by a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+vector+field"} Hamiltonian vector field
          %p
            However, this is too restrictive for a notion of a morphism. Indeed, even at the level of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+vector+space"} symplectic vector spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi W
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi: (V,\omega)\to (W,\omega')
            is required to satisfy
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo *
                  %mi ω
                  %mo ′
                  %mo =
                  %mi ω
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\phi^*\omega'=\omega.
          %p
            This implies that for any element
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∈
                    %mtext ker
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v\in\text{ker}(\phi)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi v
                    %mo ,
                    %mi w
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi w
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega(v,w) = \omega'(f(v),f(w)) = 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            is non-degenerate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            must be injective.
          %p
            Now the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph"} graph
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectomorphism"} symplectomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+submanifold"} Lagrangian submanifold
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
            space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1 \times X_2
            regarded as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            with symplectic form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi p
                      %mn 1
                      %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %msubsup
                      %mi p
                      %mn 2
                      %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_1^\ast \omega_1 - p_2^\ast \omega_2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            as above constitutes a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+correspondence"} Lagrangian correspondence
            between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_1,\omega_1)
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_2, \omega_2)
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#CattaneoMnevReshetikhin12"} Cattaneo-Mnev-Reshetikhin 12
          %p
            This suggests that instead of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifolds"} symplectic manifolds
            and whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectomorphisms"} symplectomorphisms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+correspondences"} category of correspondences
            whose objects are symplectic manifolds, and whose morphisms include
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+correspondences"} Lagrangian correspondences
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            is given by forming the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+product"} fiber product
            along adjacent legs of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondences"} correspondences
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alan+Weinstein"} Alan Weinstein
            called this would-be category the
            %em symplectic category
            and suggested that it is the natural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain"} domain
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
          %p
            However, take at face value, symplectic manifolds with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+correspondences"} Lagrangian correspondences
            between them do not quite form a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            is only well-defined when the intersection of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ∩
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_1 \times L_2 \cap X_1 \times \Delta(X_2) \times X_3
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/transversal+maps"} transverse
          %p
            Proposals for how to rectify this are in
            %a{:href => "#WehrheimWoodward"} Wehrheim &amp; Woodward
            and in
            %a{:href => "#Kitchloo"} Kitchloo
            (by turning this into an
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} (infinity,1)-category
          %h2#refinements Refinements
          %h3#PrequantumCorrespondences Prequantum correspondences
          %p
            A refinement of the symplectic category to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+geometry"} prequantum geometry
            is the following (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#SyntheticQFT"} S 13
          %p
            Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi conn
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}U(1)_{conn}
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            of smooth
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+connections"} principal connections
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Smooth%E2%88%9EGrpd"} Smooth∞Grpd
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} cohesive (∞,1)-topos
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} smooth ∞-groupoids
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Smooth
                  %mn ∞
                  %msub
                    %mi Grpd
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi conn
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Smooth\infty Grpd_{/\mathbf{B}U(1)_{conn}}
            for the corresponding
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} slice (∞,1)-topos
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Corr
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Smooth
                  %mn ∞
                  %mi Grpd
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi conn
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Corr_1(Smooth\infty Grpd,\mathbf{B}U(1)_{conn})
          %p
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+correspondences"} (∞,1)-category of correspondences
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Smooth
                    %mn ∞
                    %msub
                      %mi Grpd
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi conn
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Smooth\infty Grpd_{/\mathbf{B}U(1)_{conn}}
          %p
            An object in here is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+geometry"} prequantum geometry
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\nabla)
            given by a map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mo ∇
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi conn
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  X
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{\nabla}}
                  
                  \\\
                  \\mathbf{B}U(1)_{conn}
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            Under the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi conn
                  %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi cl
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{(-)} \colon \mathbf{B}U(1)_{conn} \to \Omega^2_{cl}
            this maps to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+structure"} presymplectic structure
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (X,\omega) = (X, F_{\nabla})
                  \\,.
          %p
            If here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            is non-degenerate, this is a symplectic structure as in Weinstein’s symplectic category.
          %p
            Moreover, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mo ∇
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mo ∇
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X_1,\nabla_1) \to (X_2,\nabla_2)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi Z
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi i
                                  %mn 1
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↘
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi i
                                  %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ⇙
                            %mi η
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mo ∇
                                  %mn 1
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ↙
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mo ∇
                                  %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi conn
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; Z
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{i_1}}\swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{i_2}}
                  \\\
                  X_1
                  \&amp;&amp; \swArrow_{\eta} &amp;&amp;
                  X_2
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}_{\mathllap{\nabla_1}}\searrow &amp;&amp; \swarrow_{\mathrlap{\nabla_2}}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; \mathbf{B}U(1)_{conn}
                  }
                  \\,,
          %p
            hence a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondence"} correspondence
            space (smooth
            = succeed "-groupoid)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            together with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} equivalence in an (∞,1)-category
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msubsup
                    %mi i
                    %mn 2
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mo ∇
                    %mn 2
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≃
                  %msubsup
                    %mi i
                    %mn 1
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mo ∇
                    %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\eta \colon i_2^\ast \nabla_2 \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} i_1^\ast \nabla_1
                  \\,.
          %p
            On the underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvatures"} curvatures
            this implies that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi i
                    %mn 2
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mi i
                    %mn 1
                    %mo *
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  i_2^\ast \omega_2 = i_1^\ast \omega_1
                  \\,.
          %p
            Hence if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z \to X \times Y
            is a maximal inclusion with this property, the above diagram is a prequantization of a morphism in the Weinstein symplectic category.
          %h3#motivic_stabilization Motivic stabilization
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nitu+Kitchloo"} Nitu Kitchloo
            defines the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} stable
            symplectic category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕊
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifolds"} symplectic manifolds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            are certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thom+spectra"} Thom spectra
          %p
            associated to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+correspondences"} Lagrangian correspondences
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi M
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ×
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{M} \times N
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi M
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{M}
            denotes the conjugate with symplectic form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\omega
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motives"} motives
          %p
            Considering an oriented version of the category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕊
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+functor"} fiber functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi M
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi 𝕊
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi pt
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : M \mapsto \mathbb{S}(pt, M)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motivic+Galois+group"} motivic Galois group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            of monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphisms"} automorphisms
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Kitchloo"} Kitchloo 12, question 8.6, p. 19
          %p
            It turns out to have a natural subgroup which is isomorphic to the quotient of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck-Teichm%C3%BCller+group"} Grothendieck-Teichmüller group
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fukaya+category"} Fukaya category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+correspondences+and+category-valued+TFT"} Lagrangian correspondences and category-valued TFT
          %h2#References References
          %p
            The way that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+correspondences"} Lagrangian correspondences
            encode
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectomorphisms"} symplectomorphisms
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+geometry"} symplectic geometry
            and hence evolution in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mechanics"} mechanics
            is reviewed (and put in the broader context of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+formalism"} BV-BRST formalism
          %ul#CattaneoMnevReshetikhin12
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alberto+Cattaneo"}> Alberto Cattaneo
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pavel+Mnev"} Pavel Mnev
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicolai+Reshetikhin"} Nicolai Reshetikhin
              %em Classical and quantum Lagrangian field theories with boundary
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1207.0239"}> arXiv:1207.0239
              )
          %p In his work on Fourier integral operators,
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lars+H%C3%B6rmander"}> Lars Hörmander
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Fourier Integral Operators I.
              %p 14
          %p following
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+Maslov"}> Victor Maslov
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Theory of Perturbations and Asymptotic Methods
          %p
            observed that, under a transversality assumption, the set-theoretic composition of two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+submanifolds"} Lagrangian submanifolds
            is again a Lagrangian submanifold, and that this composition is a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+limit"}> classical limit
            ” of the composition of certain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+operators"} linear operators
          %p Shortly thereafter,
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J%C4%99drzej+%C5%9Aniatycki"}> Jędrzej Śniatycki
              , W.M. Tulczyjew,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Generating forms of Lagrangian submanifolds
              %strong 22
              (1972) 267-275
          %p
            defined symplectic relations as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isotropic+submanifolds"} isotropic submanifolds
            of products and showed that this class of relations was closed under “clean” composition. Following in part some (unpublished) ideas of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alan+Weinstein"} Alan Weinstein
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+Guillemin"}> Victor Guillemin
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shlomo+Sternberg"} Shlomo Sternberg
              = succeed "," do
                %em Some problems in integral geometry and some related problems in microlocal analysis
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/stable/2373923"} JSTOR
          %p
            observed that the linear canonical relations (i.e., lagrangian subspaces of products of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+vector+spaces"} symplectic vector spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of this category (in which lagrangian subspaces are enhanced by halfdensities.) The quantization of the linear symplectic category was part of a larger project of quantizing canonical relations (enhanced with extra structure, such as half-densities) in a functorial way, and this program was set out more formally
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alan+Weinstein"}> Alan Weinstein
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Symplectic Manifolds and Their Lagrangian Submanifolds
                %strong 6
                (1971) 329346 [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0001-8708(71)90020-X"}> doi:10.1016/0001-8708(71)90020-X
                ]
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alan+Weinstein"}> Alan Weinstein
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Symplectic geometry
                %strong 5
                (1981) 1-13 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1090/S0273-0979-1981-14911-9"}> doi:10.1090/S0273-0979-1981-14911-9
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              W.M.Tulczyjew, S.Zakrzewski,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The category of Fresnel kernels
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0393-0440(84)90021-4"} doi
          %p Lecture notes reviewing these developments include
          %ul
            %li#Weinstein09
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alan+Weinstein"}> Alan Weinstein
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Symplectic Categories
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0911.4133"} arXiv:0911.4133
          %p from the introduction of which parts of the commented list of references above is taken. Further review includes
          %ul
            %li#Canez11
              Santiago Canez,
              %em Double Groupoids, Orbifolds, and the Symplectic Category
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1105.2592"}> arXiv:1105.2592
              )
          %p
            Further refinements in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
          %ul
            %li#WehrheimWoodward
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katrin+Wehrheim"}> Katrin Wehrheim
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+T.+Woodward"} Chris T. Woodward
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Functoriality for Lagrangian correspondences in Floer theory
                %strong 1
                2 (2010) 129-170 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.4171/qt/4"}> doi:10.4171/qt/4
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0708.2851"} arXiv:0708.2851
            %li#Kitchloo
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nitu+Kitchloo"}> Nitu Kitchloo
                ,
                %em The Stable Symplectic Category and Geometric Quantization
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1204.5720"}> arXiv:1204.5720
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nitu+Kitchloo"}> Nitu Kitchloo
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jack+Morava"} Jack Morava
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Grothendieck–Teichmüller group and the stable symplectic category
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1212.6905"} arxiv:1212.6905
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Damien+Calaque"}> Damien Calaque
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Three lectures on derived symplectic geometry and topological field theories
                %strong 25
                5 (2014) 926–947 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.indag.2014.07.005"}> doi:10.1016/j.indag.2014.07.005
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rune+Haugseng"}> Rune Haugseng
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Iterated spans and classical topological field theories
                %strong 289
                3 (2018) 1427–1488 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.0837"}> arXiv
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00209-017-2005-x"} doi:10.1007/s00209-017-2005-x
          %p
            A closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            version of the symplectic category and the observation that this hence is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic"} quantum logic
            qua
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            is in
          %ul
            %li#Slavnov05
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sergey+Slavnov"}> Sergey Slavnov
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em From proof-nets to bordisms: the geometric meaning of multiplicative connectives
                = succeed ":06" do
                  %strong 15
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sergey+Slavnov"}> Sergey Slavnov
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Geometrical semantics for linear logic (multiplicative fragment)
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tcs.2006.03.020"} doi
          %p
            Remarks about refinements to correspondences of smooth
            = succeed "-groupoids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %ul
            %li#SyntheticQFT
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Synthetic+Quantum+Field+Theory"} Synthetic Quantum Field Theory
              %a{:href => "http://cms.math.ca/Events/summer13/"} CMS Summer Meeting 2013
          %p String diagrams for the linear and affine Weinstein category using graphical linear algebra
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cole+Comfort"}> Cole Comfort
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aleks+Kissinger"} Aleks Kissinger
              = succeed "," do
                %em A Graphical Calculus for Lagrangian Relations
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.4204/EPTCS.372.24"} doi
        .revisedby
